{
  "dom_id": "step-q52004",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "For a fixed temperature, the pressure $P$ of a gas varies inversely with the volume $V$ that it occupies. When $V=3\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ the pressure is $P=2$ megapascal ($\\text{MPa}$). What will be the pressure if the gas is compressed to a container with volume $V=2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The pressure $P$ is inversely proportional to the volume $V,$ so we have\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{k}{V},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the volume is $V=3\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ the pressure is $P=2$ megapascal ($\\text{MPa}$). We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{k}{3} \\\\ 2\\cdot 3 & =k \\\\ 6 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{6}{V}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the pressure when the volume is $V=2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{6}{2}=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pressure of the gas when $V=2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}$ is $3\\,\\text{MPa}.$",
